@startuml
title Lock/Unlock bridge — where the trust actually sits
skinparam shadowing false
skinparam componentStyle rectangle
skinparam componentBackgroundColor #FDEEEE
skinparam componentBorderColor #B54A4A
skinparam packageBackgroundColor #FFFFFF
skinparam packageBorderColor #B54A4A
skinparam noteBackgroundColor #FFF6F6
skinparam noteBorderColor #B54A4A
skinparam arrowColor #B54A4A

package "Origin chain" {
  [User] as U
  [Escrow / LockProxy] as LOCK
}

package "Off-chain attestation layer" as OFF {
  [Validator / keeper / operator set] as SIG
  [Relay chain\n(Poly only)] as RELAY
}

package "Target chain" {
  [Gateway / unlock contract] as UNLOCK
  [Recipient] as R
}

U --> LOCK : 1. lock assets
LOCK --> SIG : 2. emit lock event
SIG --> RELAY : 3. include in relay state
RELAY --> UNLOCK : 4. signed header + Merkle proof
SIG --> UNLOCK : 4'. threshold signatures
UNLOCK --> R : 5. release assets

note right of OFF
  The verifier on the target chain only
  checks that enough valid signatures
  are present.

  Whoever controls the signing set can
  authorise any unlock, no matter how
  correct the on-chain code is.

  Ronin 2022: keys stolen
  Poly 2023 : keys stolen
  Ronin 2024: threshold set to zero
end note
@enduml
